# Nexus OS — gaming image
#
# A bootable container. `bootc` turns this into a system: the image is
# the source of truth, updates replace it, and the previous image stays
# on disk so the bootloader can go back.
#
# Build it in stages. Each FROM below is a checkpoint you can build and
# boot on its own, so a failure tells you which layer broke rather than
# just that something did.

# Which Fedora this is built on.
#
# Declared once, before the first FROM, so the two stages that start
# from a Fedora image cannot drift apart. A base version named in two
# places is two places that have to agree, and they eventually do not.
#
# 42 was used until September 2026 and reached end of life on 27 May
# 2026. That is not a detail: an image-mode system's whole security
# story is that updates replace it, and there is nothing left to
# update from. It also cannot be made a Fedora Remix, because the
# generic-logos package below is not built for 42.
#
# The rule this encodes: a release ships on a Fedora that is still
# supported on the day it ships, and moving to the next one is
# ordinary maintenance rather than an emergency. Fedora supports a
# release until four weeks after the one two ahead of it, so there is
# always a supported version to move to and roughly a year to do it.
#
# Overridable, for trying the next one before committing to it:
#
#     podman build --build-arg FEDORA_VERSION=45 ...
ARG FEDORA_VERSION=44

# ---------------------------------------------------------- layer order
# Why the stages below are arranged the way they are.
#
# An image is a stack of layers, and changing one invalidates every
# layer above it. Nexus used to go in near the bottom and the desktop
# -- five hundred packages, most of the image -- on top of it. So any
# change to Nexus, down to the version number in os-release, rebuilt
# the whole desktop, and pushing it meant uploading gigabytes again.
# The first push to GHCR, on 22-23 September, took all night.
#
# That cost does not stay with whoever publishes. bootc upgrade pulls
# the layers that changed, so every installed machine would have
# downloaded a whole desktop to receive a one-line fix.
#
# So the order is now: Fedora, then what rarely changes (tools, logos,
# kernel arguments), then the packages, and Nexus last. A change to
# Nexus is a few megabytes, for the person pushing and for every
# machine pulling.
#
# The trade-off, stated rather than discovered: a cached package layer
# is a stale one. Rebuilding for a Nexus change reuses the packages
# from the last time they were installed, so Fedora's security updates
# reach an image only when the package layer is deliberately rebuilt:
#
#     podman pull quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:44
#     podman build --build-arg REFRESH="$(date +%Y%m%d)" ...
#
# The pull fetches a newer fedora-bootc if there is one; REFRESH
# forces the package installs to run again. The pull is its own
# command rather than --pull=newer because that spelling only exists
# from podman 4, and Pop!_OS 22.04 ships 3.4, where --pull takes only
# true or false and rejects "newer" outright. That is a big push, and it
# is the one that carries security fixes -- see image/README.md.

# Declared once, before the first FROM, for the same reason
# FEDORA_VERSION is: a stage that needs one restates the name without
# a value and inherits this.
#
# NEXUS_VERSION has no default on purpose. The version lives in one
# place -- project(VERSION ...) in CMakeLists.txt -- and the image asks
# the binary it just built (image/identity.sh). It used to live in
# three: CMakeLists for the binary, a default here for os-release, and
# another in publish.sh for the tag. On 23 September a laptop upgraded
# to an image whose os-release said 0.1.1 while `nexus --version` in
# the same image said 0.1.0, because the build argument reached one of
# the three and not the others.
#
# Passing --build-arg NEXUS_VERSION is still allowed, as a check: the
# build fails if it disagrees with the source, rather than one of the
# two quietly winning.
#
# Empty rather than absent: with no default at all, podman prints
# "missing NEXUS_VERSION build argument. Try adding --build-arg
# NEXUS_VERSION=<VALUE>" three times on every build -- advice to bring
# back the second source of truth this exists to remove.
ARG NEXUS_VERSION=
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE=kde

# ------------------------------------------------------------- builder
# Compiling happens here and stays here. The shipped image gets the
# binary and nothing else: no compiler, no source, no build tree.
#
# This also sidesteps a real problem. Deleting a COPYed directory
# inside a RUN fails with an I/O error under rootless podman's overlay
# filesystem -- so the answer is not to delete it, but never to put it
# in the shipped layer.
FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:${FEDORA_VERSION} AS builder

# Qt 6 for Nexus Shop. Only its development files live here; the
# desktop stages already have the libraries (LXQt and KDE use them).
RUN dnf install -y \
        cmake \
        gcc-c++ \
        make \
        qt6-qtbase-devel \
        qt6-qtdeclarative-devel \
    && dnf clean all

COPY . /src

# The commit, passed in rather than read here: .containerignore keeps
# .git out of the build context, so the tree inside this stage has no
# repository to ask. Left unset, the binary reports an unknown commit
# and `nexus doctor` says so -- which is honest, but useless on an
# installed machine trying to identify itself.
ARG NEXUS_COMMIT=unknown

# NEXUS_BUILD_TESTS=OFF: the image needs the tool, not the test suite.
RUN cmake -S /src -B /build \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
        -DNEXUS_BUILD_TESTS=OFF \
        -DNEXUS_BUILD_SHOP=ON \
        -DNEXUS_COMMIT_OVERRIDE="${NEXUS_COMMIT}" \
    && cmake --build /build --parallel

# ------------------------------------------------------------- artwork
# The wallpapers, converted once. Its own stage so the converter and
# its library never reach the shipped image, and so a changed picture
# rebuilds only this and the few layers that copy from it.
#
# The originals are PNG in image/artwork/wallpapers, one per profile,
# named after it. image/wallpaper/make-wallpapers.py says what it
# refuses and why.

FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:${FEDORA_VERSION} AS artwork

RUN dnf install -y python3-pillow google-noto-sans-fonts && dnf clean all

COPY image/wallpaper/make-wallpapers.py /usr/local/bin/make-wallpapers
COPY image/artwork/wallpapers /artwork/wallpapers

RUN python3 /usr/local/bin/make-wallpapers /artwork/wallpapers /wallpapers

# The installer's branding, drawn here and carried in the image so that
# image/build-installer.sh takes it from the very image it installs.
# See image/installer-branding.py.
COPY image/installer-branding.py /usr/local/bin/installer-branding
RUN python3 /usr/local/bin/installer-branding /installer

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- os
# Fedora plus the things that almost never change. Not a target:
# nothing ships from here directly.

FROM quay.io/fedora/fedora-bootc:${FEDORA_VERSION} AS os

# Changing this reruns every install below -- the package refresh
# described above. Its value is only ever printed; it exists to be
# different from last time.
ARG REFRESH=none

# Fedora's Count Me off (0.1.86, outside review round 3, P3). It is an
# anonymous weekly count -- the Fedora version and that the machine is
# up, no ID -- sent by rpm-ostree's timer and by dnf when it refreshes
# repositories. Anonymous, but still a report nobody asked for, and
# Nexus says it sends none. Fedora's own documented opt-out for bootc
# images, plus a dnf5 override so repositories added later (RPM Fusion
# at first boot) are not counted either. The override is in /usr, the
# distribution's place, so updates carry it and a person can still
# overrule it in /etc. Before the first dnf below, so the builds are
# not counted. To turn it back on, as on any Fedora:
#     printf '[*]\ncountme=1\n' | sudo tee /etc/dnf/repos.override.d/80-nexus-no-countme.repo
#     sudo systemctl unmask --now rpm-ostree-countme.timer
# (and countme=1 back in /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo for rpm-ostree).
RUN set -eu; \
    sed -i -e 's/^countme=1/countme=0/' /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo; \
    mkdir -p /usr/share/dnf5/repos.override.d; \
    printf '# Nexus: no Count Me for any repository (see README).\n[*]\ncountme=0\n' \
        > /usr/share/dnf5/repos.override.d/80-nexus-no-countme.repo; \
    systemctl mask rpm-ostree-countme.timer; \
    if grep -l '^countme=1' /etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo; then \
        echo "Count Me still on in the files above"; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "Count Me: off"

# Privacy defaults that would otherwise be Fedora's and Mozilla's
# (0.1.86, outside review round 3). Both are plain files, read by the
# programs themselves; nothing runs.
#
# Firefox: telemetry, studies and ad measurement ("privacy-preserving
# attribution") locked off; sponsored tiles, sponsored suggestions and
# promotions off but not locked, so anyone can turn them back on in
# Settings. Safe Browsing and tracking protection stay as Mozilla ships
# them. /etc/firefox/policies is read by every Firefox on the system
# and wins over a copy in Firefox's own directory. about:policies shows
# what is active.
COPY image/firefox/policies.json /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json
# NetworkManager: the machine's name is not sent to every network it
# joins, and IPv6 uses temporary addresses. See the file.
COPY image/network/30-nexus-privacy.conf \
     /usr/lib/NetworkManager/conf.d/30-nexus-privacy.conf

# Nexus reads repository metadata, which arrives compressed in
# whichever format the tool that wrote it preferred. Fedora uses zstd
# for small repositories and zchunk for large ones, so an image that
# ships Nexus has to ship the decompressors or it can only read the
# small ones.
RUN echo "Package refresh: ${REFRESH}" \
    && dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
        zchunk \
        zstd \
    && dnf clean all

# Container images by digest (0.1.81). The weekly build resolves every
# tag Nexus pulls (image/pins/images.list) to its digest just before
# this build (image/pin-images.py); `nexus` reads the result and pulls
# exactly those bytes. A local build has no pinned-images file and
# pulls by tag, as before -- the line below says which this is.
# Server backups hold TLS and ACME keys and whole worlds: root's only,
# set at every boot, so machines with older 0644 archives are covered
# too (0.1.85, outside review N2).
RUN printf 'd /var/lib/nexus/backups 0700 root root -\n' > /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/nexus-backups.conf

COPY image/pins/ /usr/share/nexus/pins/
RUN if [ -s /usr/share/nexus/pins/pinned-images ]; then \
        echo "Pins: $(grep -vc '^#' /usr/share/nexus/pins/pinned-images) container images by digest"; \
    else \
        echo "Pins: none (local build) -- containers are pulled by tag"; \
    fi

# Fedora's artwork out, generic artwork in.
#
# Fedora's trademark guidelines let anyone build on Fedora and say so,
# but not ship Fedora's marks. fedora-logos is where those marks live
# on a running system: the boot splash, the login screen, the icon
# every desktop shows for "about this computer". Keeping it while
# calling the result Nexus-CORE is using somebody else's trademark to
# label a different product -- which matters more, not less, once a
# release is sold.
#
# generic-logos is the package Fedora provides for exactly this. Both
# provide system-logos, so the swap is one package for another rather
# than a hole where the artwork was.
#
# Not available for Fedora 42, which is one of the reasons the base
# above moved.
#
# The rpm -q is the point of the whole block. dnf can report success
# on a transaction that resolved differently than intended, and an
# image that quietly kept Fedora's logos would look exactly like one
# that did not. This asks the machine what it actually has, and fails
# the build if the answer is wrong.
RUN set -eu; \
    if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        dnf swap -y fedora-logos generic-logos; \
    else \
        echo "fedora-logos was not installed; installing generic-logos"; \
        dnf install -y generic-logos; \
    fi; \
    if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos is still installed; refusing to ship it." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rpm -q generic-logos; \
    dnf clean all

# RPM Fusion, enabled on every image.
#
# Fedora ships only what it can legally and freely ship, so Steam, the
# NVIDIA driver and full video codecs live in RPM Fusion, a third-party
# repository run since 2008 alongside Fedora and pointed to by Fedora's
# own documentation. Without it the gaming profile installed gamemode
# and nothing else on the Asus on 23 September: `steam` resolved to
# nothing, and so does gaming's NVIDIA preference, nvidia-driver-libs.
#
# Only the repositories are enabled here -- two small packages that
# carry the .repo files and signing keys. Nothing from RPM Fusion is
# installed into the image itself; it arrives when somebody chooses a
# profile that asks for it, and Nexus names the source when it does.
#
# Chosen over Flatpak Steam (24 September): a native package integrates
# with controllers, drives and the rest of the system without a sandbox
# in the way, and updates with everything else.
#
# Known limit, not fixed by this: the NVIDIA driver is a kernel module.
# On an image-based system it cannot be built on the machine at update
# time the way akmods does on ordinary Fedora, and with Secure Boot on
# an unsigned module will not load. NVIDIA needs a build-time answer.
RUN dnf install -y \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
    && rpm -q rpmfusion-free-release rpmfusion-nonfree-release \
    && dnf clean all

# Let somebody mistype the disk passphrase.
#
# systemd-cryptsetup gives up after three attempts. On a machine
# encrypted by default that is the most likely thing a real user gets
# wrong, and giving up does not look like giving up: the boot carries
# on for about five minutes emitting dracut warnings about a device
# that is never going to appear, ends in "Could not boot", and drops
# to an emergency shell whose own advice -- press Ctrl-D -- starts a
# job that waits forever for the same device.
#
# tries=0 means keep asking. There is nothing to protect by refusing:
# the disk is the thing guarding itself, and an attacker with the
# machine can reboot as many times as they like. The limit only ever
# punishes the owner.
#
# In kargs.d rather than passed to the installer, so it is a property
# of the image: it arrives on machines installed from this ISO and on
# machines that upgrade into it, without anyone having to remember.
#
# Verified 22 September 2026: in a VM, three wrong passphrases were
# followed by a fourth prompt, and /proc/cmdline carried the option.
RUN mkdir -p /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d \
    && printf '%s\n' \
        '# Keep asking for the disk passphrase instead of giving up' \
        '# after three attempts. See image/Containerfile.' \
        'kargs = ["rd.luks.options=tries=0"]' \
        > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/10-nexus-luks.toml

# ----------------------------------------------------- desktop-packages
# The desktop, before Nexus. Not a target on its own; `desktop` below
# is this plus Nexus.
#
# KDE on Wayland. Chosen over GNOME because gaming benefits from
# variable refresh and per-display scaling, which KDE has handled
# better for longer.
#
# --exclude=rootfiles: it ships /root/.bash_logout, and in a bootc
# image /root is managed by ostree rather than being an ordinary
# directory. RPM cannot unpack into it, and one failed unpack aborts
# the entire transaction.
#
# --setopt=install_weak_deps=False: Recommends are what somebody else
# thought you might like. An image should contain what it needs.
#
# The desktop comes from the profile, not from this file, and which
# profile is a build argument rather than a decision baked in here.
#
#     podman build --build-arg NEXUS_PROFILE=minimal ...
#
# One pipeline, several machines. That is the claim the whole design
# rests on, and hardcoding one profile here would leave it untested.
#
# The lists are generated by image/generate-lists.sh and committed:
# a build you cannot read is a build you cannot audit, and every line
# of those files says which capability it answers. Installing them
# does not need the nexus binary -- the list is already written --
# which is what lets this stage sit below Nexus.

FROM os AS desktop-packages

ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

# Kept rather than deleted, in /usr/share so the built image carries
# the list it was built from. That is worth a few kilobytes: an image
# that cannot say what it is made of is one nobody can check.
#
# Deleting it is also not possible. Removing a COPYed directory inside
# a RUN fails with an I/O error under rootless podman's overlay
# filesystem -- the same failure that killed the first build of the
# base stage, reintroduced here and caught the same way.
COPY image/generated /usr/share/nexus/lists

RUN set -eu; \
    list="/usr/share/nexus/lists/${NEXUS_PROFILE}.rpm.list"; \
    if [ ! -f "$list" ]; then \
        echo "No generated list for profile '${NEXUS_PROFILE}'." >&2; \
        echo "Have:" >&2; \
        ls /usr/share/nexus/lists >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    packages="$(grep -v '^#' "$list" | awk 'NF {print $1}')"; \
    echo "Installing ${NEXUS_PROFILE}:"; \
    echo "$packages"; \
    dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
        --disablerepo='rpmfusion-*' \
        --disablerepo=fedora-cisco-openh264 \
        $packages; \
    dnf clean all

# Whether this machine boots to a desktop depends on whether the
# profile asked for one.
#
# minimal has a session and deliberately no display manager: you log
# in on a console and start it yourself. Enabling sddm here assumed
# every profile wanted the same machine, which is the assumption the
# build argument exists to remove.
RUN set -eu; \
    manager=""; \
    for candidate in sddm gdm lightdm; do \
        if systemctl list-unit-files "${candidate}.service" \
               --no-legend 2>/dev/null | grep -q .; then \
            manager="${candidate}"; \
            break; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    if [ -n "$manager" ]; then \
        echo "Display manager: ${manager}"; \
        systemctl enable "${manager}.service"; \
        systemctl set-default graphical.target; \
    else \
        echo "No display manager installed; booting to a console."; \
        systemctl set-default multi-user.target; \
    fi

# A session nobody can start is worse than none on the login screen.
#
# The minimal edition is Wayland only (labwc), but LXQt still ships its
# X11 session entry; with no X server installed, choosing it would drop
# back to the login screen with no explanation. So X11 entries go when
# there is no X server to run them.
RUN set -eu; \
    if [ ! -x /usr/bin/Xorg ] && [ -d /usr/share/xsessions ]; then \
        for entry in /usr/share/xsessions/*.desktop; do \
            [ -e "${entry}" ] || continue; \
            echo "No X server; hiding X11 session ${entry}"; \
            printf '\nHidden=true\nNoDisplay=true\n' >> "${entry}"; \
        done; \
    fi

# The minimal edition's desktop, finished (28 September, from David's
# VM test of 0.1.18): labwc as the compositor, and a panel with
# launchers in it. A script rather than lines here; see its comments.
# It does nothing on an image without LXQt.
COPY image/lxqt-defaults.sh /usr/libexec/nexus/lxqt-defaults
RUN sh /usr/libexec/nexus/lxqt-defaults

# Fedora's artwork must still be gone.
#
# The os stage swapped it out, but that proves nothing about this
# one: a desktop is hundreds of packages, and any of them may require
# fedora-logos by name rather than the system-logos both packages
# provide. dnf would install it without comment, and the image would
# ship Fedora's marks again with the earlier check still reading as
# passed -- a check that looked at the base and said nothing about
# the machine.
#
# If this fails, the message names the package that wanted it, which
# is the thing to fix.
RUN if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos came back in the desktop stage." >&2; \
        echo "Required by:" >&2; \
        rpm -q --whatrequires fedora-logos >&2 || true; \
        exit 1; \
    fi

# Only Fedora's own packages go into the image.
#
# RPM Fusion is enabled in the os stage, so every dnf install after it
# could choose RPM Fusion's builds -- and did: 0.1.3 to 0.1.7 shipped
# fdk-aac from rpmfusion-nonfree, and openh264 from Cisco's repository,
# found reading a build log on 25 September. Both are about patents,
# not code: Cisco's licence covers openh264 only when each user
# downloads it from Cisco, which is why Fedora's own images carry the
# noopenh264 stub instead; and RPM Fusion's nonfree builds are meant to
# be fetched by the user's machine, not redistributed inside someone
# else's image. So image builds disable both repositories, and the
# extras step below refuses the build if anything from them got in.
# They stay enabled on installed machines: Steam and codecs are then
# downloaded by the user, which is the arrangement they are offered
# under.
#
# The login screen's theme and the boot splash.
#
# Both were missing, and both were noticed on the first machine. SDDM
# was installed without a theme -- weak dependencies are off, and the
# Breeze theme is one -- so the login screen was SDDM's bare fallback.
# And there was no Plymouth, so booting scrolled systemd's "[ OK ]"
# lines and asked for the disk passphrase as a line of text, where
# Pop!_OS and Fedora show a splash with a password box.
#
# Three more found by running Steam on the Asus, 25 September, and
# none of them Steam's alone:
#
#   glibc-all-langpacks  the base ships only the minimal langpack, so
#                      en_US.UTF-8 did not exist. Every program asking
#                      for it fell back to plain ASCII; Steam tried to
#                      build it itself and failed. All languages rather
#                      than English: the installer now asks which one,
#                      and it cannot add packages to an image, so
#                      whatever somebody picks has to be here already.
#   pciutils           lspci. Steam, and anyone reporting a hardware
#                      problem, expects it.
#   pulseaudio-utils   pactl, which talks to PipeWire's PulseAudio
#                      side. Steam uses it to find audio devices.
#
# And zram-generator-defaults (27 September): swap compressed in RAM,
# as Fedora's own desktops have it. The biggest single help on a 2 GB
# machine, and harmless on a big one.
#
# sddm-breeze only comes with Plasma. It is Plasma's own theme and
# pulls Plasma's libraries in with it, which is the last thing the
# minimal edition wants; that edition keeps SDDM's own theme.
#
# Here, below the wallpapers and above Nexus, rather than in the
# package list: a Nexus change must not rebuild these, and adding them
# here does not rebuild the few hundred packages above.
RUN set -eu; \
    extra=""; \
    if rpm -q sddm plasma-workspace > /dev/null 2>&1; then extra="sddm-breeze"; fi; \
    dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
        --disablerepo='rpmfusion-*' \
        --disablerepo=fedora-cisco-openh264 \
        plymouth \
        plymouth-scripts \
        plymouth-theme-spinner \
        glibc-all-langpacks \
        pciutils \
        pulseaudio-utils \
        flatpak \
        zram-generator-defaults \
        ${extra}; \
    dnf clean all; \
    if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos came back with the splash or login theme." >&2; \
        rpm -q --whatrequires fedora-logos >&2 || true; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    shipped="$(rpm -qa --qf '%{NAME} %{VENDOR}\n' | grep -i 'rpm fusion' \
        | grep -v '^rpmfusion-[a-z]*-release ' || true)"; \
    for name in openh264 mozilla-openh264; do \
        if rpm -q "${name}" > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
            shipped="${shipped} ${name}"; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    if [ -n "${shipped}" ]; then \
        echo "The image would ship packages it must not redistribute:" >&2; \
        echo "${shipped}" >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "Redistribution: Fedora packages only"

# OpenGL ES for the compositor (0.1.62). KDE Plasma 6.8.2 arrived with
# Fedora's updates on 1 November, and its KWin opens libGLESv2.so.2 at
# start instead of linking it -- so no package requires it, and with
# weak dependencies off nothing brought it in. KWin crashed at start
# ("Couldn't open libGLESv2.so.2", core dump in EglContext), the login
# screen never appeared, and the Asus showed a black screen on 0.1.61.
# A few hundred kilobytes; the build now fails if any compositor in
# the image cannot find it, so the next such change stops here and not
# on somebody's laptop.
RUN set -eu; \
    dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False libglvnd-gles; \
    dnf clean all; \
    ldconfig; \
    ldconfig -p | grep -q 'libGLESv2\.so\.2 '; \
    for compositor in /usr/bin/kwin_wayland /usr/bin/labwc; do \
        [ -x "${compositor}" ] || continue; \
        echo "OpenGL ES: found for ${compositor}"; \
    done

# Flathub's remote definition, carried in the image.
#
# The gaming profile takes Steam from Flathub (see its Flatpak: line),
# and nexus setup adds the remote from this file. Fetched here, when the
# image is built, so the signing key Flathub's packages are checked
# against arrives with the system -- signed along with it -- rather
# than from whatever answers on a user's network. Not enabled until a
# profile needs it: an image that adds remotes nobody asked for is
# making choices for people.
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /usr/share/nexus; \
    curl -fsSL --proto =https --proto-redir =https --retry 3 -o /usr/share/nexus/flathub.flatpakrepo \
        https://dl.flathub.org/repo/flathub.flatpakrepo; \
    grep -q '^GPGKey=' /usr/share/nexus/flathub.flatpakrepo; \
    echo "Flathub remote definition: /usr/share/nexus/flathub.flatpakrepo"

# The splash has to be inside the initramfs, because the passphrase is
# asked for before the real system is mounted. The initramfs in a
# bootc image is built with the image, not on the machine, so it is
# rebuilt here now that Plymouth is present. --no-hostonly because
# the image boots on machines this build has never seen; ostree
# because without it the initramfs cannot find the deployment to boot.
#
# bgrt is Fedora's own choice: the maker's logo from the firmware, a
# spinner, and the password box. The Nexus mark can replace the
# spinner's watermark later.
#
# rhgb and quiet are what turn the scrolling "[ OK ]" lines into the
# splash. They are in kargs.d, like the LUKS setting, so they belong
# to the image. Esc during boot still shows the lines.
#
# Rebuilding the initramfs is the one step here that can stop a
# machine booting, so what it produced is checked before it is kept:
# the splash, the passphrase prompt, and the piece that finds the
# deployment. A missing one fails the build, not somebody's boot.
RUN set -eu; \
    plymouth-set-default-theme bgrt; \
    kver="$(ls /usr/lib/modules)"; \
    if [ "$(printf '%s\n' "${kver}" | wc -l)" -ne 1 ]; then \
        echo "Expected one kernel in /usr/lib/modules, found:" >&2; \
        echo "${kver}" >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    dracut --no-hostonly --reproducible --force \
        --kver "${kver}" --add "ostree plymouth" \
        "/usr/lib/modules/${kver}/initramfs.img"; \
    chmod 0600 "/usr/lib/modules/${kver}/initramfs.img"; \
    contents="$(lsinitrd "/usr/lib/modules/${kver}/initramfs.img")"; \
    for needed in plymouthd systemd-cryptsetup ostree-prepare-root; do \
        if ! printf '%s\n' "${contents}" | grep -q "${needed}"; then \
            echo "The rebuilt initramfs has no ${needed}; refusing it." >&2; \
            echo "Without it this image would not boot an encrypted disk." >&2; \
            exit 1; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    echo "Splash: bgrt, in the initramfs for ${kver}"; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '# Boot splash instead of scrolling log lines. See image/Containerfile.' \
        'kargs = ["rhgb", "quiet"]' \
        > /usr/lib/bootc/kargs.d/20-nexus-splash.toml

# The time zone: chosen in the installer, and automatic only if asked.
#
# nexus-timezone sets it by hand, looks it up once, or turns automatic
# lookups on and off. Automatic is off unless somebody runs
# `sudo nexus-timezone auto on`: a lookup asks a server where this IP
# address is, and that is the user's decision, not the image's. The
# NetworkManager hook does nothing until then.
COPY image/timezone/nexus-timezone /usr/bin/nexus-timezone
COPY image/timezone/90-nexus-timezone \
     /usr/lib/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/90-nexus-timezone
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/bin/nexus-timezone \
        /usr/lib/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/90-nexus-timezone

# When dnf cannot change this system, say what can (0.1.63). /usr is
# part of the image, so `sudo dnf install x` fails with Fedora's own
# error and no next step (the Asus, 1 November). In interactive shells
# dnf still runs exactly as typed; only when an install, remove or
# upgrade FAILS does one hint follow (`sudo nexus install x`, ...).
# `command sudo dnf ...` bypasses it entirely.
COPY image/dnf-hint/nexus-dnf-hint.sh /etc/profile.d/nexus-dnf-hint.sh
RUN chmod 0644 /etc/profile.d/nexus-dnf-hint.sh

# The clock: network time on and the hardware clock in universal time,
# once per machine. The installer leaves network time off when it had
# no network, and the Asus came out reading its hardware clock as local
# time -- a day behind, next to Linux Mint (12 October). See the script.
COPY image/clock/nexus-clock /usr/libexec/nexus/clock
COPY image/clock/nexus-clock.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-clock.service
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/clock \
    && systemctl enable nexus-clock.service

# The clock catches up whenever it is wrong (0.1.61), not only just
# after starting.
#
# Fedora's chrony.conf has `makestep 1.0 3`: the clock is set straight
# only in chrony's first three updates; after that a wrong clock is
# only slewed -- sped up or slowed by at most 1/12 -- so a laptop that
# wakes from sleep days behind takes weeks to catch up. The Asus sat
# three days behind on 1 November with network time "on", and Firefox
# refused every HTTPS site (their certificates looked not yet valid).
# `makestep 1.0 -1`: set it straight whenever it is more than a second
# off. Right for a laptop that sleeps; a jump of the clock is what
# such a laptop needs, and nothing on a desktop depends on it never
# jumping.
#
# Changed in the image's /etc/chrony.conf, so machines that update get
# it -- unless somebody edited their own chrony.conf, which is kept.
RUN set -eu; \
    conf=/etc/chrony.conf; \
    rpm -q chrony > /dev/null || \
        { dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False chrony && dnf clean all; }; \
    test -f "${conf}"; \
    if grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*makestep[[:space:]]' "${conf}"; then \
        sed -Ei 's/^[[:space:]]*makestep[[:space:]].*/makestep 1.0 -1/' "${conf}"; \
    else \
        printf '%s\n' '' \
            '# Nexus: set the clock straight whenever it is over 1 s off.' \
            'makestep 1.0 -1' >> "${conf}"; \
    fi; \
    [ "$(grep -Ec '^[[:space:]]*makestep[[:space:]]' "${conf}")" = 1 ]; \
    grep -qx 'makestep 1.0 -1' "${conf}"; \
    echo "chrony: $(grep -x 'makestep 1.0 -1' "${conf}")"

# A firewall, on (0.1.56). Until 24 October there was none: firewalld
# was not even installed, so anything listening was reachable by
# everyone on the same network -- harmless at home, not on a cafe's or
# a university's Wi-Fi (found by `nexus server` on the Asus: "Firewall:
# not running, so every port is open to your network").
#
# The zone is "public" with what a desktop needs coming in: DHCPv6,
# finding printers and other devices (mdns), Windows shares on the
# network (samba-client) and KDE Connect. Not Fedora Workstation's own
# zone, which opens every port above 1024. `nexus server` opens exactly
# the ports its servers use, and closes them when one is removed.
#
# Each step only when needed: firewall-offline-cmd fails (exit 16,
# ZONE_ALREADY_SET) when asked for what is already so, and that failed
# the weekly build on 24 October.
#
# Set in the image's /etc: on a machine that updates, new files there
# arrive with the update, so existing machines get the firewall too.
RUN set -eu; \
    rpm -q firewalld > /dev/null || \
        { dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False firewalld && dnf clean all; }; \
    [ "$(firewall-offline-cmd --get-default-zone)" = public ] || \
        firewall-offline-cmd --set-default-zone=public; \
    for service in dhcpv6-client mdns samba-client; do \
        firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --query-service="${service}" > /dev/null || \
            firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --add-service="${service}"; \
    done; \
    if firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --query-service=ssh > /dev/null; then \
        firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --remove-service-from-zone=ssh; \
    fi; \
    systemctl enable firewalld.service

# (--remove-service-from-zone, not --remove-service: firewall-offline-cmd
# reads --remove-service as the old lokkit option, which cannot be
# combined with --zone -- "Can't use lokkit options with other
# options", the failed builds of 8 November.)
#
# No remote login by default (0.1.70). Fedora's base image starts sshd,
# and the public zone let SSH in, so on the Asus (8 November) anyone
# on the same Wi-Fi could try passwords. A desktop does not need that
# door. Both closed above and here; turning it on is one line, and
# `nexus doctor` shows the state:
#     sudo systemctl enable --now sshd
#     sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=ssh --permanent && sudo firewall-cmd --reload
RUN if systemctl list-unit-files sshd.service > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        systemctl disable sshd.service; \
    fi

# Nexus first in the start list, once per machine: corrects the dead
# "Fedora" entry bootupd writes for the other system's EFI partition
# when Nexus is installed next to another Linux. See the script.
COPY image/boot-entry/nexus-boot-entry /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-entry
COPY image/boot-entry/nexus-boot-entry.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-boot-entry.service
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-entry \
    && systemctl enable nexus-boot-entry.service

# Signed updates. See image/signing/README.md.
#
# The policy is merged, not replaced: everything under
# ghcr.io/davidosdas-official must carry a signature made with the
# Nexus key; every other registry keeps whatever Fedora set. The
# machine only enforces this when its origin says ostree-image-signed:
# -- the installer sets that; older machines switch once by hand.
#
# No public key, no build: an image whose policy points at a missing key
# would refuse every update it is ever offered.
COPY image/signing/cosign.pub /etc/pki/containers/nexus-core.pub
COPY image/signing/nexus-core.yaml /etc/containers/registries.d/nexus-core.yaml
COPY image/signing/merge-policy.py /usr/libexec/nexus/merge-policy
RUN set -eu; \
    grep -q "BEGIN PUBLIC KEY" /etc/pki/containers/nexus-core.pub; \
    python3 /usr/libexec/nexus/merge-policy

# Updates: checked once a day, installed only when asked (decided
# 4 October). Nothing is downloaded or changed until the person runs
# `sudo nexus update --apply`, and the machine never restarts by itself.
#
# The check is Nexus's own: a timer in each person's session (desktop
# stage) compares the image this machine runs with the one on the
# registry and sends a notification. rpm-ostree's automatic check is
# off: on an image-based machine it looks only at added packages,
# refreshing every repository's metadata each day to do it, and it
# missed a new Nexus image (Asus, 4 October). bootc's timer is masked:
# it downloads, applies and restarts on its own, and on a machine with
# any added package it fails without a word ("cannot upgrade via
# bootc", Asus, 4 October).
RUN set -eu; \
    conf=/etc/rpm-ostreed.conf; \
    if [ -f "${conf}" ] && grep -q 'AutomaticUpdatePolicy=' "${conf}"; then \
        sed -i 's/^#\?AutomaticUpdatePolicy=.*/AutomaticUpdatePolicy=none/' "${conf}"; \
    fi; \
    systemctl disable rpm-ostreed-automatic.timer 2> /dev/null || true; \
    systemctl mask bootc-fetch-apply-updates.timer; \
    echo "Updates: checked by Nexus, applied only when asked."

# The boot menu: five seconds instead of Fedora's one, so somebody can
# see it and pick the version before an update (David, Asus,
# 4 October: "a user will need to be hella fast"). Fedora's grub.cfg is
# generated and may be rewritten by its updates; the custom.cfg it
# reads at the end is ours. Written once, at the first start of this
# image; a person who changes or removes the line keeps their choice.
COPY image/boot-menu/nexus-boot-menu /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-menu
COPY image/boot-menu/nexus-boot-menu.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-boot-menu.service
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-menu \
    && systemctl enable nexus-boot-menu.service

# The wallpapers: one per profile, all of them in the picker.
#
# Below the packages and above Nexus, because a picture changes less
# often than the tool and far more often than Fedora. A new wallpaper
# rebuilds from here down -- a few megabytes -- and leaves the
# packages above cached.
#
# Which one a person sees is decided at their login, by
# apply-wallpaper: the add-on they chose at first boot, else this
# image's profile, else base. Plasma's own default cannot do that. It
# is one setting for the whole image, fixed when the image is built,
# and the whole point is that choosing gaming should look like it.
#
# The login screen gets base. It is shown before anybody has logged
# in, so there is no one's choice to follow, and /usr cannot be
# changed on a running machine to follow it anyway.
COPY --from=artwork /wallpapers/ /usr/share/wallpapers/
COPY --from=artwork /installer/product.img /usr/share/nexus/installer/product.img
COPY image/wallpaper/apply-wallpaper /usr/libexec/nexus/apply-wallpaper
COPY image/wallpaper/nexus-wallpaper.desktop \
     /etc/xdg/autostart/nexus-wallpaper.desktop

RUN set -eu; \
    chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/apply-wallpaper; \
    login="$(ls /usr/share/wallpapers/nexus-base/contents/images/*.jpg | head -n 1)"; \
    test -f "${login}"; \
    if ! command -v plasma-apply-wallpaperimage > /dev/null && \
       ! command -v pcmanfm-qt > /dev/null; then \
        echo "Neither Plasma nor LXQt in this profile; wallpapers are in"; \
        echo "the image but nothing will apply them at login."; \
    fi; \
    themed=0; \
    for candidate in breeze maldives; do \
        if [ -f "/usr/share/sddm/themes/${candidate}/theme.conf" ]; then \
            mkdir -p /etc/sddm.conf.d; \
            printf '[Theme]\nCurrent=%s\n' "${candidate}" > /etc/sddm.conf.d/10-nexus.conf; \
            echo "Login theme: ${candidate}"; \
            break; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    for theme in /usr/share/sddm/themes/*/; do \
        if grep -q '^background=' "${theme}theme.conf" 2>/dev/null; then \
            printf '[General]\nbackground=%s\n' "${login}" \
                > "${theme}theme.conf.user"; \
            echo "Login background: ${theme}"; \
            themed=1; \
        fi; \
    done; \
    if [ "${themed}" -eq 0 ]; then \
        echo "No SDDM theme with a background setting; the login"; \
        echo "screen keeps its own."; \
    fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Nexus
# The same few layers end both `base` and `desktop` below: the binary,
# first boot, profiles, and what the system says it is. They are
# written out twice because a Containerfile has no way to include one
# block in two stages; image/identity.sh holds the logic, so what is
# repeated is only which files go where. Keep the two copies the same,
# except the profile list, which desktop narrows on purpose.
#
# The first-boot unit is in every target rather than only the last
# one: a --target build stops wherever it is told, and the unit once
# simply did not exist in the desktop image, which looked exactly like
# systemd refusing to run it.
#
# Every target ends by reinstalling the SELinux policy. dnf leaves the
# policy store inconsistent during a container build -- a module listed
# with its files missing -- and nothing notices until a deployment is
# finalized on a real machine, where refreshing the policy fails and
# takes the deployment with it. Reinstalling puts the files back;
# `semodule -B` cannot, because it renames directories and overlayfs
# cannot rename across layers. (The empty extra_varrun module the build
# also leaves is removed at first boot, where the filesystem allows it.)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- base
# Fedora, the tools and Nexus, with no desktop. What
# image/generate-lists.sh runs `nexus image` inside.

FROM os AS base

ARG NEXUS_VERSION
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

COPY --from=builder /build/cli/nexus /usr/bin/nexus
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot /usr/bin/nexus-first-boot
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-first-boot.service
COPY components/profiles /usr/share/nexus/profiles
COPY components/aliases /usr/share/nexus/aliases
COPY image/identity.sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

RUN NEXUS_VERSION="${NEXUS_VERSION}" NEXUS_PROFILE="${NEXUS_PROFILE}" \
    sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

ENV NEXUS_PROFILES=/usr/share/nexus/profiles
ENV NEXUS_ALIASES=/usr/share/nexus/aliases

RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

# -------------------------------------------------------------- desktop
# What ships: the desktop packages, then Nexus on top.

FROM desktop-packages AS desktop

ARG NEXUS_VERSION
ARG NEXUS_PROFILE

# KDE Connect's ports only where KDE Connect is (0.1.82, outside review:
# they were opened on every machine, installed or not). Machines whose
# zone file was changed locally keep what they have; `nexus security`
# names the leftover.
RUN if rpm -q kdeconnectd > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --query-service=kdeconnect > /dev/null || \
            firewall-offline-cmd --zone=public --add-service=kdeconnect; \
        echo "Firewall: kdeconnect (KDE Connect is installed)"; \
    else \
        echo "Firewall: no kdeconnect (KDE Connect is not installed)"; \
    fi

# What `nexus update` shows as the new version comes from the
# nexus.version label, added by the build command (--label) from
# CMakeLists.txt: NEXUS_VERSION is empty here on purpose (see above),
# so a LABEL written here came out empty in 0.1.32 and the Asus saw
# "a newer Nexus image" instead of a number (4 October).

# The daily "update available" notification, in each person's session
# (see "Updates" in the os stage). Switched on in /usr, which every
# update replaces whole, rather than /etc, which is merged. (0.1.31 put
# this in the server stage by mistake; desktops never got it.)
# notify-send comes from libnotify.
COPY image/updates/nexus-update-notify.service \
     image/updates/nexus-update-notify.timer \
     /usr/lib/systemd/user/
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/systemd/user/timers.target.wants; \
    ln -sf ../nexus-update-notify.timer \
        /usr/lib/systemd/user/timers.target.wants/nexus-update-notify.timer; \
    rpm -q libnotify > /dev/null || \
        { dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False libnotify && dnf clean all; }

COPY --from=builder /build/cli/nexus /usr/bin/nexus

# Nexus Shop (0.1.37: step 1, reading only). Desktops only: this is
# the desktop stage, not base, which starts the same way. Its app
# lists: Fedora's from appstream-data (14 MB), Flathub's downloaded by
# the Shop itself once a day (flatpak update --appstream, no
# password). The Qt libraries are already here for the desktop; the
# rpm -q only makes sure, as for libnotify above.
COPY --from=builder /build/gui/shop/nexus-shop /usr/bin/nexus-shop
COPY image/shop/nexus-shop.desktop /usr/share/applications/nexus-shop.desktop
COPY image/shop/nexus-shop.svg /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/nexus-shop.svg

# The brightness keys (0.1.38): bigger steps and a bar on screen. The
# keys themselves are pointed here by lxqt-defaults (new users) and by
# this user unit, once, for users who already had their own copy.
COPY image/brightness/nexus-brightness /usr/bin/nexus-brightness
COPY image/brightness/nexus-brightness-keys.service /usr/lib/systemd/user/
RUN set -eu; \
    chmod 0755 /usr/bin/nexus-brightness; \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/systemd/user/default.target.wants; \
    ln -sf ../nexus-brightness-keys.service \
        /usr/lib/systemd/user/default.target.wants/nexus-brightness-keys.service
RUN set -eu; \
    missing=""; \
    for package in appstream-data qt6-qtdeclarative; do \
        rpm -q "${package}" > /dev/null || missing="${missing} ${package}"; \
    done; \
    if [ -n "${missing}" ]; then \
        dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False ${missing} && dnf clean all; \
    fi; \
    rpm -q appstream-data qt6-qtdeclarative
# RPM Fusion's app lists (0.1.41), so the Shop can show its apps beside
# Fedora's and Flathub's. Not fatal: without them the Shop simply shows
# the other sources, and its Sources page says which it has.
RUN set -u; \
    for package in rpmfusion-free-appstream-data rpmfusion-nonfree-appstream-data; do \
        rpm -q "${package}" > /dev/null || \
            dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False "${package}" || \
            echo "Not available: ${package} (the Shop shows the other sources)"; \
    done; \
    dnf clean all; \
    ls /usr/share/swcatalog/xml/
# Calls (0.1.59): baresip, the open-source calling program (SIP) that
# Pop!_OS also offers, in the image so people can build on it -- from
# a terminal or from Python -- without adding anything first. On Nexus
# adding a package means a restart, so it is here already. Small, and
# nothing of it runs until somebody starts it.
#
# Opus for the sound. For the speakers and microphone the PipeWire
# module when Fedora has one, otherwise PulseAudio's, which PipeWire
# also speaks.
RUN set -eu; \
    dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False baresip baresip-opus; \
    dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False baresip-pipewire || \
        { echo "No baresip-pipewire; using baresip-pulse"; \
          dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False baresip-pulse; }; \
    dnf clean all; \
    rpm -q baresip baresip-opus
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot /usr/bin/nexus-first-boot
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-first-boot.service \
     /usr/lib/systemd/system/nexus-first-boot.service
# Desktops only (0.1.51): when nothing was chosen at the first start,
# one notification after login says where to choose, with a button
# that opens the question in a terminal. Switched on in /usr, like the
# update notification.
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-setup-reminder /usr/libexec/nexus/setup-reminder
COPY image/first-boot/choose-add-ons /usr/libexec/nexus/choose-add-ons
COPY image/first-boot/nexus-setup-reminder.service \
     image/first-boot/nexus-setup-reminder.timer \
     /usr/lib/systemd/user/
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/setup-reminder /usr/libexec/nexus/choose-add-ons && \
    mkdir -p /usr/lib/systemd/user/timers.target.wants && \
    ln -sf ../nexus-setup-reminder.timer \
        /usr/lib/systemd/user/timers.target.wants/nexus-setup-reminder.timer
# Back to the version that worked, by itself (0.1.72). At the first
# start of a new system version, if neither the login screen nor a
# desktop is up within 150 seconds, Nexus makes the previous version
# the default again and restarts (the KDE 6.8 black screen of
# 1 November). Machines that boot to a console are not checked.
COPY image/boot-check/nexus-boot-check /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-check
COPY image/boot-check/nexus-boot-check.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/boot-check && \
    systemctl enable nexus-boot-check.service

# Desktops only (0.1.61): when the time zone is still the installer's
# default (America/New_York, nothing chosen), one notification after
# login says so, with a button that opens `nexus-timezone pick`.
# Nothing is looked up online. Gone once a zone is set or kept.
COPY image/timezone/nexus-timezone-reminder /usr/libexec/nexus/timezone-reminder
COPY image/timezone/choose-timezone /usr/libexec/nexus/choose-timezone
COPY image/timezone/nexus-timezone-reminder.service \
     image/timezone/nexus-timezone-reminder.timer \
     /usr/lib/systemd/user/
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/libexec/nexus/timezone-reminder /usr/libexec/nexus/choose-timezone && \
    ln -sf ../nexus-timezone-reminder.timer \
        /usr/lib/systemd/user/timers.target.wants/nexus-timezone-reminder.timer
# Only what can be added to this desktop -- the one place this block
# differs from base's copy, on purpose.
#
# minimal, tiling and server are not additions. Each is a different
# machine: choosing server here gave a KDE desktop with an SSH server
# on it, and minimal or tiling gave KDE plus a second desktop, the
# opposite of what either asks for. First boot lists whatever is in
# this directory, so leaving them out is what takes them off the
# list. They come back as images of their own once they are done.
#
# A profile added to components/profiles is not offered here until it
# is added to this list. That is deliberate: it is the moment to ask
# whether it belongs on this desktop.
#
# kde is not offered either: it is this image, and listing it only
# ever said "already satisfied". showcase and the minimalism look come
# back once they exist; image/check-add-ons.sh checks that everything
# listed here can really be installed.
#
# Which add-ons depends on the edition, so they are copied to a pool
# and the edition's own set is picked from it below. (Copied, not
# deleted: removing a COPYed file in a RUN fails under rootless podman.)
# The minimal edition does not offer gaming: Steam alone wants more
# memory than a machine that edition is for has.
COPY components/profiles/base.profile \
     components/profiles/development.profile \
     components/profiles/gaming.profile \
     components/profiles/hosting.profile \
     components/profiles/media.profile \
     components/profiles/school.profile \
     components/profiles/cybersecurity.profile \
     components/profiles/vpn.profile \
     /usr/share/nexus/profile-pool/
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /usr/share/nexus/profiles; \
    case "${NEXUS_PROFILE}" in \
        minimal) offered="base cybersecurity development hosting media school vpn" ;; \
        *)       offered="base cybersecurity development gaming hosting media school vpn" ;; \
    esac; \
    for name in ${offered}; do \
        cp "/usr/share/nexus/profile-pool/${name}.profile" /usr/share/nexus/profiles/; \
    done; \
    echo "Add-ons offered (${NEXUS_PROFILE}):" $(ls /usr/share/nexus/profiles | sed 's/\.profile$//' | grep -v '^base$')
COPY components/aliases /usr/share/nexus/aliases
COPY image/identity.sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

RUN NEXUS_VERSION="${NEXUS_VERSION}" NEXUS_PROFILE="${NEXUS_PROFILE}" \
    sh /usr/libexec/nexus/identity

ENV NEXUS_PROFILES=/usr/share/nexus/profiles
ENV NEXUS_ALIASES=/usr/share/nexus/aliases

RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

# Not what ships. It still stacks its packages above Nexus, so a
# gaming image pays the old cost on every change; worth the same
# reordering if it is ever released.
FROM desktop AS gaming

# Fedora bootc ships no 32-bit multilib. Steam needs it, so it is added
# explicitly rather than assumed -- see decisions 4 and 5.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        mesa-dri-drivers.i686 \
        mesa-vulkan-drivers.i686 \
        mesa-libGL.i686 \
        mesa-libEGL.i686 \
        glibc.i686 \
        libgcc.i686 \
        alsa-lib.i686 \
        pulseaudio-libs.i686 \
        libX11.i686 \
        libXext.i686 \
        libXinerama.i686 \
        libXrandr.i686 \
        libXScrnSaver.i686 \
        nss.i686 \
    && dnf clean all

# RPM Fusion carries Steam. Enabling a third-party repository is a
# decision, not a detail: it is the only non-Fedora source in the
# image, and it is here because Steam is not in Fedora proper.
RUN dnf install -y \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
        "https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/nonfree/fedora/rpmfusion-nonfree-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm" \
    && dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        steam \
        gamemode \
        gamescope \
        mangohud \
        vulkan-tools \
    && dnf clean all

# Controllers. A gamepad that does not work is a bug a beginner cannot
# describe, so the drivers and udev rules are present from first boot.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        kernel-modules-extra \
        libratbag-ratbagd \
    && dnf clean all

# Audio: PipeWire is already default on Fedora. Named here so the image
# states it rather than inheriting it silently.
RUN dnf install -y \
        --exclude=rootfiles \
        pipewire \
        pipewire-pulseaudio \
        wireplumber \
    && dnf clean all

# --------------------------------------------------------------- nexus
# Rebuild the SELinux policy store.
#
# dnf writes into the store during a container build and what comes
# out is inconsistent: a module listed with its files missing. Nothing
# notices until a deployment is finalized on a real machine, where the
# policy is refreshed, fails, and takes the deployment with it.
#
# At the end of every stage a build can stop at, not once at the
# start: --target desktop never reaches the later stages, and a store
# rebuilt before six more installs is a store rebuilt too early.
# Remove a policy module that has no files.
#
# A container build leaves extra_varrun registered in the store as an
# empty directory. semodule cannot read past it -- not to rebuild the
# policy, not even to list modules -- so finalizing a deployment on a
# real machine fails and the deployment is discarded. That is what
# stopped rpm-ostree from layering a package, and it looked like
# every other kind of problem first.
#
# A module with no files provides no policy, so removing it loses
# nothing. With it gone, semodule reads 429 modules and layering
# works: packages survive a reboot and the machine stays atomic.
# (removed at first boot instead -- see first-boot/nexus-first-boot:
#  overlayfs refuses to delete it during a build, and it deletes
#  cleanly on a real filesystem.)

# Restore the SELinux policy files dnf left incomplete.
#
# A container build can leave a policy module listed with its files
# missing -- extra_varrun, in this case. Nothing notices until a
# deployment is finalized on a real machine, where refreshing the
# policy fails and takes the deployment with it, which is what
# stopped rpm-ostree from layering a package.
#
# Reinstalling puts the files back. Rebuilding the store with
# semodule -B would be the direct fix and cannot work here: it swaps
# directories by renaming them, and overlayfs cannot rename across
# layers.
RUN dnf reinstall -y selinux-policy-targeted && dnf clean all

FROM gaming AS final

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="Nexus-CORE"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="A Linux system that explains its own decisions"
LABEL containers.bootc="1"

# Nothing is installed in this stage, so there is no policy store to
# repair here: the gaming stage above ends with the reinstall, and
# this stage adds only labels. Three more reinstalls used to sit
# below, each rebuilding a store that nothing had touched and each
# adding a layer worth a couple of hundred megabytes.
#
# They arrived by the same route as the duplicated comment blocks
# above them -- a patch script that checked whether its work was done
# by looking for a line that was already there, decided it was not,
# and appended a second copy. Three times.

# And once more at the very end, for the stages after desktop: the
# gaming stage adds RPM Fusion, a second set of packages that knows
# nothing about this decision.
RUN if rpm -q fedora-logos > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo "fedora-logos came back after the desktop stage." >&2; \
        rpm -q --whatrequires fedora-logos >&2 || true; \
        exit 1; \
    fi

# Last, so that it lints the image that ships.
#
# bootc requires this to succeed for the image to be considered
# valid, and an instruction after it is an instruction it never saw.
RUN bootc container lint
